    pr_ref:  # 6745
    description: "A static, per-function test selector built on hypergumbo's own call graph, scored on the WI-damom test-bed against coverage-directed selection (WI-gapos) and today's file-level slice.\n\nTODAY. smart-test's static selection is already a hypergumbo reverse slice: `hypergumbo slice --files <changed>` \"finds all symbols in these files and performs reverse slice to identify dependent files\". Seeding with ALL symbols of a changed file and answering in FILES makes it file-level twice over: a one-line edit to one io_boundary.py function selects every file that reaches any io_boundary.py symbol, and then every test file mapped to those files.\n\nTHE CHANGE. Seed the reverse slice with only the CHANGED blocks (block_hash.py's per-block digests already identify them: digest moved, or block vanished/renamed per selection_index.changed_blocks), and answer in test FUNCTIONS (or test files containing a reaching test function) rather than dependent source files.\n\nWHY BOTHER WHEN COVERAGE EXISTS. Coverage is the stronger signal locally, but it needs history:\n1. CI has no local selection index, so a per-function CI manifest can only come from a static selector. (Narrowing the CI manifest still needs owner sign-off: the owner's 2026-08-16 option A on WI-kuliv / WI-bolot kept it static and file-level.)\n2. Cold start and new tests: no coverage history yet; the graph knows what a test calls as soon as it is written.\n3. Cross-check on the index: a test the graph says reaches a changed block but the index never observed running it suggests a stale index — keep the test, refresh the index.\n4. Dogfooding: every test the oracle says must run that the graph misses is a hypergumbo call-graph bug with a ready-made repro.\nNOT a fix for the ~45% of wall-clock the index cannot speak about (measure-narrowing-headroom.py): that is 15.9% producing no coverage rows (tracker package outside COV_PATHS; subprocess and scripts/ tests leave no in-process trace) plus tests never run locally. The direct fixes are subprocess coverage (COVERAGE_PROCESS_START), adding the tracker package to COV_PATHS, and index freshness; a CLI-subprocess test reaches nearly everything in the graph anyway.\n\nSOUNDNESS RULE (the hard part). A missing edge here drops a test, the one unacceptable outcome. Python hides callers from a static graph through registries, registration decorators, dispatch tables, string lookups (getattr; the `explain` edge-type lookup from WI-kuliv), pytest fixtures bound by parameter name, monkeypatch, parametrize over function objects, import-time code, and YAML catalogues that change behaviour with no code change. So: select per function only when EVERY reverse path from a test to the changed block is resolved and above a confidence floor; if any path crosses an unresolved or low-confidence edge, or the changed function is referenced from a dispatch table / registry, fall back to the file-level rule for that file. hypergumbo already records edge resolution and confidence, so the check is cheap; how often it falls back on hypergumbo's own code (likely often in the linkers and all_analyzers) is unmeasured.\n\nDone when: the selector runs on the WI-damom test-bed, its must/extra table sits beside the coverage selector's and the file-level baseline's, it drops no must-select test on any scripted edit, and the fallback rate on hypergumbo itself is measured (share of changed blocks that fall back, on a sample of recent commits)."  # 6745
    fields: {}  # 6745
- op: discuss  # 8cdf
